{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:21:15Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 141, "kind": "message", "text": "## Report\n\n**What happened:** Round 9 built undo-delete-with-toast (Taiga #13). Build took 3 attempts and review took 3 passes: review-1 found a blocking crash, review-2 found a blocking flicker bug in the same function, review-3 approved with only non-blocking follow-ups. Test pass confirmed everything held.\n\n**What cost time or quality:**\n\nThe flicker bug review-2 caught (`build-frontend-attempt2.output`, `review-attempt2.output`) was not introduced by the attempt-2 fix \u2014 I checked `build-frontend-attempt1.log:99`, and the buggy ordering was already present in the very first implementation: `deferDelete`'s `setTimeout` callback called `setPendingDeleteIds(...)` (removing the pending-delete flag) *before* `await api.deleteTask(id)`, in the same function review-1 was already reading closely enough to catch the co-located crash bug (`tasks.find` returning `undefined`). Review-1's report (`review-attempt1.output`) never mentions this second issue \u2014 it inspected the function for the one failure mode it found and stopped. That gap cost an entire extra build+review round-trip (attempt 2, ~370s of build + ~430s of review per the log timestamps) that a single more-thorough pass over `deferDelete` would have avoided.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n`agents/reviewer.md`, Alan Heitkotter's lens (\"failure paths and concurrency\"): added a bullet naming this exact pattern \u2014 a state commit landing before an `await` it should wait on inside an async callback \u2014 plus an explicit instruction to trace *every* state mutation in an async callback against its `await` points once you're already in there, not just the one bug already suspected. This is scoped to the concrete, evidenced failure (state-before-await ordering), not a vague \"be more thorough\" instruction.\n\n**What I deliberately did not change:**\n\n- The repeated \"npm run lint blocked by sandbox approval\" note restated verbatim across all three build attempts and the test pass \u2014 mildly wasteful, but each dispatch is a stateless fresh agent per the shared conventions, and the note is one line each time, not a real cost.\n- Review-2's and review-3's verdicts themselves \u2014 both were correctly calibrated (blocking for a genuine AC3 violation, approve-with-followups for genuinely optional nits). No sign of severity inflation or softened blockers.\n- Build attempts 2 and 3 \u2014 both were tight, scoped fixes that named root cause, not symptom, and correctly stayed in `App.jsx` only. Nothing to correct in `agents/developer.md` from this round's evidence.\n\n**Needs a human:** nothing \u2014 this round's slippage was a reviewer-thoroughness gap, fully within my remit, and the fix is the one instruction change above.\n\nVERDICT: done", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "bb2c07c1-ebce-406f-bea6-1d2e18b5d6c3"}}
